      "completion_tokens": 1173,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance. Its scientific name is **dihydrogen monoxide**, but it is most commonly known by the formula **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is made of and why it is unique:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nWater is a molecule composed of two elements:\n*   **Hydrogen:** A gas that makes up about 10% of the universe.\n*   **Oxygen:** A gas that makes up about 21% of the air we breathe.\n\nWhen two hydrogen atoms bond with one oxygen atom, they form a water molecule.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at the temperatures normally found on the planet:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** The form we drink and use for washing.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible steam).\n\n### 3. Key Properties\nWater is unique because of its molecular structure, which makes it \"polar.\" This means the molecule has a slight positive charge on one end and a slight negative charge on the other. This polarity gives water several special characteristics:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for dissolving nutrients in our bodies and transporting them to cells.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick tightly to one another, creating a \"skin\" on the surface. This allows small insects to walk across puddles without sinking.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life. It is the primary component of all living things (humans are about 60% water). It is necessary for:\n*   Transporting nutrients and oxygen to cells.\n*   Removing waste products.\n*   Regulating body temperature.\n*   Lubricating joints and protecting tissues."
